            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Voltaire's Early Life</strong><br>\nFrançois-Marie Arouet, known as Voltaire, was born in Paris in 1694. He was educated by the Jesuits at the Collège Louis-le-Grand, where he learned Latin and Greek; later he became fluent in Italian, Spanish, and English. Early in his career, he decided to adopt the pen name \"Voltaire.\" He was known for his wit and became a writer, starting with poetry and then plays.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Career</strong><br>\nVoltaire's writings were diverse, including plays, poems, novels, essays, and historical and scientific works. Over 20,000 letters and more than 2,000 books and pamphlets are attributed to him. He was an outspoken advocate for civil liberties and was often critical of the French establishment. His works often challenged the status quo and led to periods of exile and imprisonment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exile to England</strong><br>\nAfter a series of imprisonments and confrontations with the French authorities, Voltaire was exiled to England in 1726. During his three-year stay, he was influenced by Britain's constitutional monarchy, its support for the arts and sciences, and its relative freedom of speech. This experience shaped his ideas on civil liberties and freedom, which he later expressed in his works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
