          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Voltaire and The Enlightenment</strong><br>\nVoltaire became a central figure in the Enlightenment, a European intellectual movement that advocated reason as the primary source of authority and legitimacy. His writings emphasized the importance of scientific observation and the use of reason. He was a crusader against tyranny, bigotry, and cruelty, contributing to the period's revolutionary thinking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Candide</strong><br>\nOne of Voltaire's most famous works is \"Candide,\" published in 1759. This satirical novella criticizes the optimism prevalent in society during his time. The story follows Candide, a young man disillusioned by the hardships of the world, which contradicts the teachings of his mentor, Pangloss, who insists that \"all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Views</strong><br>\nVoltaire was a deist, believing in a God who created the universe but does not interfere in human affairs. His views on religion were complex; he respected Jesus's moral teachings but was sharply critical of the Christian clergy, dogmatism, and religious intolerance. He championed religious freedom and the separation of church and state.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
