          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Advocacy for Justice and Freedom</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Voltaire campaigned for justice and the rights of individuals. He often took up high-profile cases, advocating for victims of political and religious discrimination. He helped secure the release of Jean Calas and others wrongfully convicted, emphasizing the need for fair trials and the rule of law.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Frederick the Great</strong><br>\nVoltaire spent periods at the court of Frederick the Great, King of Prussia, a relationship that combined friendship and intellectual exchange. However, their relationship was strained by mutual suspicion and Voltaire's fear of Frederick's authoritarian tendencies. Yet, this connection provided Voltaire with a platform for his ideas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Voltaire's Legacy</strong><br>\nVoltaire died in Paris in 1778 but left behind a legacy as one of the greatest writers of the Enlightenment. His advocacy for freedom of speech, separation of church and state, and civil rights has had a lasting impact. Voltaire's critical and questioning mindset has continued to influence thinkers, writers, and reformers for centuries.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
